.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt ZEXPLORE-API 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zexplore-api ,
.Nm zexplore-txn
.Nd let a virtual machine snapshot, roll back and commit its own ZFS volume, and nothing else
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zexplore-api
.Op Fl -unix Ar path
.Op Fl -vsock Ar port
.Op Fl -tcp Ar host : Ns Ar port
.Nm zexplore-api
.Fl -selftest
.Nm zexplore-txn
.Op Fl -cid Ar cid
.Op Fl -port Ar port
.Op Fl -unix Ar path
.Op Fl -tcp Ar host : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl -vm Ar vm
.Op Fl -json
.Cm begin | rollback | commit | list
.Sh DESCRIPTION
ZFS snapshots are nearly instant, so
.Dq snapshot, try something, roll back or keep it
can be a transaction: a database migration undone in milliseconds, a test
machine reset to its golden state.
The host owns the volumes, so
.Nm zexplore-api ,
on the host, does it for a guest that asks, and only to that guest's own
volumes.
.Pp
Each request is one line of JSON.
The daemon works out who is asking \(em a guest by the vsock address its
connection comes from, matched to the libvirt domain with that address; a
local caller as root, or by a per-machine token \(em and allows only the
caller's primary volume,
.Ar base Ns Pa / Ns Ar vm ,
and its data volumes,
.Ar base Ns Pa / Ns Ar vm Ns Pa - Ns Ar name ;
a volume that another domain's name claims belongs to that domain.
When libvirt cannot be asked, a data volume is refused.
Every operation is appended to the audit log.
.Pp
.Nm zexplore-txn ,
in the guest, asks:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm begin
Snapshot the volume and open a transaction.
.It Cm rollback
Roll the volume back to the transaction's snapshot.
.It Cm commit
Keep the change and drop the snapshot.
.It Cm list
This machine's open transactions.
.El
.Pp
.Pa zexplore-api.service
listens on
.Pa /run/zexplore-api.sock
and vsock port 9455.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -unix Ar path , Fl -vsock Ar port , Fl -tcp Ar host : Ns Ar port
Where to listen, or where
.Nm zexplore-txn
connects; TCP accepts only a token.
.It Fl -selftest
Check the volume scope rule against invented domain lists, touching no
socket, ZFS or libvirt, and exit.
.It Fl -cid Ar cid
The host's vsock address; default 2.
.It Fl -vm Ar vm
Name the machine outright; root on the unix socket only.
.It Fl -json
The reply as it came.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev ZEXPLORE_VM_BASE
Where the machines' volumes are; default
.Pa rpool/vms .
.It Ev ZEXPLORE_TXN_STATE
Transactions and tokens; default
.Pa /var/lib/kldload/zexplore .
.It Ev ZEXPLORE_AUDIT
Default
.Pa /var/log/kldload/zexplore-txn.log .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, with this release's
.Nm zexplore-api
first on the path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zexplore-api --selftest
selftest: 14/14 cases hold
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr zfs-rollback 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Rolling back a volume the guest has mounted and is writing corrupts it.
Keep the data being rolled back on a separate volume and unmount it
first, or roll the operating system's own volume back by restarting the
machine onto it.
The daemon refuses to roll back a volume a running machine has open
unless forced, and otherwise trusts the guest to have stopped writing.
